# Overview
#
# `shape` names the outline of an object. Docs borrow it for an
# interface, a type, or a wire format (`a Duck-shaped value`, `the same
# shape as the v1 response`), and the reader must guess which one the
# writer meant. The fix names it, so `Duck-shaped` becomes `implements
# Duck` and `the same shape as the v1 response` becomes `the same fields
# as the v1 response`. The verb (`two pressures shape the design`) and
# the idiom `in good shape` hide the same detail, so the rule reports
# them too. The rule sits at Recommended, because it still reports some
# uses of `shape` for the size of a tensor in machine-learning docs.
#
# Expression
#
# `word` holds `shape` and `shapes`, and `main` also matches `shaped`.
# `literal` lists the tensor and array nouns, such as `tensor`, `array`,
# and `matrix`. `shape` passes after a word in `literal` (`the tensor
# shape`). It also passes before `of`, a determiner, and a word in
# `literal` (`shape of the tensor`). A determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. Harper's
# part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word class,
# and the pattern names the tag for a determiner (`DET`).
#
# Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before `shape`
# and narrows the finding back to `shape`. A sentence that opens with
# `Shape` therefore passes. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token,
# so the `of` guard sits glued to `shape` and reads the tokens right
# after it.
#
# False Positives
#
# `shaping` stays out, because `traffic shaping` keeps it as a term of
# art. Tensor and array docs use `shape` for the size of each dimension,
# so `shape` after `tensor`, `array`, or `matrix` passes, and so does
# `shape of the tensor`.
#
# The rule still reports `output shapes` and `the same shape` in
# machine-learning docs, where nearly every hit names the size of a
# tensor. The measure.py corpus holds no machine-learning docs, so no
# sample counts that misfire.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 14 lines in the measure.py corpus. In 12 of 14 sampled
# hits, the word stood for a structure or a format, or it was the verb
# or an idiom. One misfire named a geometric figure (`a set of possible
# shapes that also includes Circle`). The other misfire named the curve
# that the `shape` field of a scheduler plugin configures.
expr literal [tensor, tensors, array, arrays, matrix, matrices, ndarray]
expr word [shape, shapes]
expr main [<(!@literal @word!(( )of( )DET( )@literal)), @word>, shaped]
let message "`shape`. Name the interface, type, or format it stands for."
let description "Flags `shape` and `X-shaped` where they stand in for an interface, a type, or a format."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The tensor shape must match the weight matrix."
allows "Check the array shape before the call."
allows "The shape of the tensor must match the weights."
allows "The router applies traffic shaping to egress."
allows "Reshape the array before the call."
allows "The mock implements Duck."
